WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.080
Too weak, too slow.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:07.440
Magnus Carlsen is talented, confident,&nbsp;
and -- arrogant. And he knows it.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.360
The thing is, I’m so arrogant that even&nbsp;
when he was mating me I didn’t realize it.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.000
When asked who his greatest rival in chess was.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.560
He said: No one.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.160
Writing on X: What am I&nbsp;
supposed to do? Make stuff up?

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.280
Let’s connect on X, I’m&nbsp;
@Newsthink - link in description!

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.360
He did list his second-greatest rival as&nbsp;
American chess grandmaster Hikaru Nakamura

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.760
We’re simply better at chess than&nbsp;
the others and so we can make good

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.640
moves more quickly, so they need to spend time.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.360
Carlsen is so confident in his abilities that,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.560
with time ticking away at a world championship&nbsp;
event, he literally took a sip of water!

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.600
And then…he spent more time&nbsp;
adjusting his chess pieces!

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.520
But beneath the bravado lies a child prodigy.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:58.280
By the age of two, he completed&nbsp;
a 50-piece jigsaw puzzle.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.480
When he was four, he had memorized the names of&nbsp;
most of his native Norway’s 356 municipalities.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.120
By eight, chess had become his life.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.040
Initially, he really just wanted&nbsp;
to beat his oldest sister.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.396
And then he was hooked, devouring chess&nbsp;
strategy books in his dad’s library.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.600
It took him only a year to beat&nbsp;
his dad Henrik for the first time.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.160
He credits skiing as his secret weapon&nbsp;
to winning, helping him mentally reset:

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.840
You went in the morning for skiing. Frankly,&nbsp;
I don’t think I would have won today if I

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:40.880
hadn’t done that because I was just sick&nbsp;
of the hotels and terrible air everywhere.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.760
Apart from skiing, soccer, and yoga help him keep

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.080
in peak physical and mental shape to handle&nbsp;
the rigorous demands of chess tournaments.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.628
He says most chess games hinge on the final&nbsp;
hours, where mistakes are made due to fatigue.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.320
The number of possible moves&nbsp;
in chess is greater than the

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.840
number of atoms in the universe!&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:07.400
Most of the time I know what to&nbsp;
do. I don’t have to figure it out.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.720
He’s been called the Mozart of chess.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:15.560
Much like the composer’s inexplicable&nbsp;
genius, Carlsen’s skills seem otherworldly.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.520
In 2004, when Carlson was just 13, he&nbsp;
stunned the world by beating former

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.160
champion Anatoly Karpov at&nbsp;
a tournament in Reykjavik.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.240
The following day, Carlsen came close to beating&nbsp;
the number one ranked player, Gary Kasparov.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.600
Kasparov barely managed to escape with a draw&nbsp;
in the first game and then won the second.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.760
The match had a profound effect on Carlsen&nbsp;
who, a month later, became a grandmaster - the

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.600
highest title awarded to chess players&nbsp;
by the International Chess Federation.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.920
Kasparov briefly became his&nbsp;
coach a few years down the road.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.880
Paying hundreds of thousands of&nbsp;
dollars for training was a good

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:57.200
investment because Carlsen reached&nbsp;
world No. 1 at the age of 19.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.160
He has been ranked number one non-stop since 2011.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.000
The International Chess Federation recognizes him

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.600
as “arguably the best player&nbsp;
in the history of chess.”

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.760
He has a peak Elo rating of 2882 – the&nbsp;
highest ever recorded in the system used

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.040
in competitive chess to rate how good&nbsp;
you are based on tournament results.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:21.680
Chess.com lists him as the second&nbsp;
greatest player of all time,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.840
behind Gary Kasparov. Do you&nbsp;
agree? Let me know in the comments.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.000
Even those who aren’t avid followers of the&nbsp;
game recognize Carlsen’s name.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.960
He's achieved celebrity status. That was quick!

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.240
One of Carlsen’s biggest assets&nbsp;
is his remarkable memory.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.120
60 Minutes once produced a segment in&nbsp;
which he played ten opponents at the

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.160
same time while not looking at the chess boards.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.040
He had to memorize the positions on all&nbsp;
the boards and keep track of them in his

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.720
mind as they changed.
He won all ten games.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.160
A good memory is a tremendous&nbsp;
asset in chess as it lets you

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.840
navigate the early game with confidence,&nbsp;
recalling countless opening strategies.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:08.000
It's like having a playbook in your mind,&nbsp;
ready to deploy any game plan on the spot.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.920
But perhaps his biggest&nbsp;
strength is his versatility.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:18.440
Grandmaster Vishy Anand said “He can be tactical.&nbsp;
He can be positional. He can be many things.”

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.960
Kind of like a dancer who can gracefully glide

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.241
from ballet and burst into the&nbsp;
spirited moves of salsa.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.440
He defeated Anand to become the World&nbsp;
Chess Champion for the first time in 2013.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.200
Unbelievable. Magnus Carlsen!
Champion of the world.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:38.440
This signaled the rise of a new era of players&nbsp;
who grew up in the age of online chess.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.200
Although Carlsen has benefitted from&nbsp;
competing against players online and

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.080
getting access to instant game analysis 
advantages legends like Bobby Fischer never

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.240
had - he’s been pretty clear that computers&nbsp;
themselves are boring to play against.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:58.160
He refused to play the chess engine&nbsp;
Mittens, calling it "a soulless” computer.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.040
Yet the might of machines is undeniable.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.560
Kasparov famously lost against the IBM

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:07.920
supercomputer Deep Blue in&nbsp;
a series of matches in 1997.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.640
In the final game, he was&nbsp;
crushed in just over an hour.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.880
So, while computers stand as formidable&nbsp;
opponents in the realm of chess,

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:20.160
does any human stand a chance&nbsp;
against the might of Magnus Carlsen?

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.760
Chess players are believed to&nbsp;
peak between 35 and 45 years old.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:28.880
Carlsen is 32 going on 33&nbsp;
at the time of this video.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:31.760
It’s hard to believe he may&nbsp;
not have reached his prime.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:35.440
Even harder to believe is that he&nbsp;
may be tired of playing the game.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.280
He decided not to defend his title at&nbsp;
the World Chess Championship in 2023,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.080
explaining in his podcast:

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:56.400
I am not motivated to play another&nbsp;
match. I simply feel that I don’t

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:04.720
have a lot to gain. I don’t particularly like it.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.920
For the five-time Champion, it&nbsp;
was akin to abdicating his crown.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:19.240
I don’t rule out a return in the future. But&nbsp;
I wouldn’t particularly count on it.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:24.160
He’s also taken a liking to poker though&nbsp;
he says he has no grand ambitions there.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.400
For someone who likes to win above everything,&nbsp;
don’t count him quite out of chess just yet.

